) VALUES (
  '27ab49b9-f634-4ac9-a5c0-380e278381e0',
  'silva-international-spy-thriller',
  'International Spy Thriller',
  1,
  'In a world that has been broken by history, the operative''s job is not to win but to restore — to return stolen things to the people from whom they were taken, one careful act at a time.',
  'Literary but propulsive: the prose carries genuine elegance — a well-made sentence, an exact image, a paragraph that earns its description — inside an action architecture that never loses momentum. Chapters are longer than the short-chapter thriller norm, but each chapter earns its length by advancing plot and deepening character simultaneously. The pacing is a slower burn in the opening third: the world is established, relationships are rendered with emotional precision, the geography carries political and historical weight before the acceleration begins. Once the threat is established, the pace tightens without sacrificing the prose quality — Silva trusts the reader to hold both. Dialogue is economical and loaded; what is not said is often the sentence that matters. The restoration metaphor is structural: the work of cleaning and revealing an old master, layer by patient layer, runs as a throughline against the operational action, not as decoration but as moral grammar.',
  ARRAY[
    'The art world as operational cover — the protagonist''s expertise in restoration is not a personality quirk; it is the mechanism by which she moves through the world without triggering the alarms that a known intelligence operative would trigger. Galleries, auction houses, private collections, Vatican restoration workshops — these are genuine operational environments, and the technical vocabulary of conservation (ground layers, varnish removal, inpainting, provenance gaps) does double duty as craft detail and plot architecture.',
    'European geography as moral landscape — each city the protagonist moves through carries specific historical and political weight that is made explicit in the prose: Rome carries the weight of the Vatican and its institutional compromises; Vienna carries the weight of the war and what was hidden during it; Venice carries the weight of beauty that has survived by accommodating power. The setting is never merely atmospheric; it is a character with a history that the protagonist must navigate.',
    'The Holocaust as undertow — without being didactic, the historical trauma of the twentieth century shapes the protagonist''s moral framework and the antagonist''s ideological roots. Stolen art, hidden identities, institutional silence — these are not background details but the structural conditions from which the plot grows. The protagonist''s mandate is partly restoration in the literal sense: returning what was taken.',
    'The support network as emotional architecture — the mentor figure, the team members, the spouse — these relationships carry genuine weight across the narrative; they are not plot devices but people whose history with the protagonist creates stakes that the mission alone cannot generate. When one of them is endangered, the reader has been given enough shared history that the threat lands as personal loss, not plot mechanics.',
    'The villain''s coherent worldview — the antagonist operates from an internally consistent ideological position: religious conviction, nationalist grievance, institutional loyalty — the motivation is historically grounded and morally serious even when the methods are not. The reader should be able to reconstruct the antagonist''s argument without endorsing it. The thriller villain who is simply evil is a lesser creature; Silva''s antagonists are wrong about what the world requires, not wrong about what the world has done.',
    'The restoration metaphor as chapter rhythm — intersperse the operational chapters with moments of literal restoration work: the protagonist in front of a canvas, removing centuries of varnish, revealing what is underneath. These passages slow the pace deliberately and earn their length by functioning as moral counterpoint — the patient, irreversible work of uncovering truth against the violent, contingent work of intelligence operations.'
  ],
  'History: present and specific, not decorative — the date of a particular atrocity, the name of a particular institution''s failure, the title of a particular stolen painting and what happened to it — these facts anchor the thriller in a world that actually occurred and give the plot stakes that extend beyond the individual mission. The institutional church: complex and implicated rather than simply corrupt — the Vatican''s historical accommodations, its genuine theology, its current political calculations — rendered without cartoon villainy or naive exoneration. Russian intelligence: professional, patient, and ideologically serious — not comic-book adversaries but people whose institutional culture and operational methods the protagonist understands and respects even in opposition. The protagonist''s own agency: small, deniable, and morally accountable in ways that larger agencies are not — the intimacy of the team creates both the warmth and the vulnerability.',
  ARRAY[
    'Genre-thriller pacing throughout — the slower burn of the opening act is not a flaw to be corrected but a feature of the form; the reader who is given time to inhabit the world will feel the acceleration of the plot more acutely when it arrives',
    'Art-world detail as decoration — every element of conservation, provenance, and art history must do narrative work: establish setting, reveal character, or plant a plot element; if it does none of these, cut it',
    'Villain incompetence — the antagonist''s plan must be operationally sound; the protagonist wins because of superior information or a calculated risk, not because the villain made an avoidable error',
    'suddenly — the plot''s acceleration should be visible in retrospect from chapter two onward; if a development arrives without preparation, the architecture has failed',
    'Holocaust as backdrop only — the historical weight must be active in the plot, not atmospheric; a character, a painting, a document, a building should carry the specific history into the present action',
    'Romantic subplot that resolves cleanly — the protagonist''s personal relationships carry wounds and complications that the mission does not heal; the love story is real but the world it lives in is not safe; resolution is partial at best',
    'Interior monologue as therapy — the protagonist processes experience through action and observation, not self-analysis; emotional weight is carried in what she notices and what she chooses not to say',
    'Omniscient protagonist — the protagonist is formidable but not infallible; she should be genuinely surprised once, genuinely at risk once, and require the support network to survive at least one complication she could not anticipate',
    'Institutional reform as ending — the corrupt institution is damaged but not dismantled; the specific bad actors are removed; the conditions that produced them remain; the protagonist knows this and continues anyway',
    'Generic European city — each city must be named, each named city must carry specific history that intersects the plot; a scene set in Vienna must be set in Vienna, not in a generic Old City with a cathedral and a cafe'
  ],
  ARRAY[
    'She worked in the Galleria Borghese''s conservation laboratory three mornings a week, which was how she preferred to spend her time in Rome when she was not doing the other thing. The canvas before her was a minor Caravaggio — minor only in the sense that it had never been famous, not in the sense of its quality, which was considerable — and it had been sleeping under two centuries of varnish that had done it no favors. She used a cotton swab and a solvent mixture she had spent six months refining. The varnish came away in yellowed rings, and underneath it the saint''s robe was the exact red of arterial blood, which Caravaggio had understood before anyone else that this was what it looked like. She made a note in her restoration log and did not think about the meeting she had at noon. There was time for one thing at a time. This was the one thing for now.',
    'In Vienna the past was not past. It was the building on the Ringstrasse that had been a cultural institution before the war and a storage facility during it and a cultural institution again after, with no acknowledgment of the interval. It was the provenance gap in the auction catalog — 1938 to 1953, no record of ownership — which was not an absence of information but a form of information in itself. Cross had learned to read provenance gaps the way a restorer read pentimento: the earlier composition showing through, the original image that the later work was painted over but could not quite suppress. The painting in question was not large. It had hung in a drawing room in the Leopoldstadt before a family that no longer existed had been required to leave everything behind. It was hanging now in the residence of a man who had purchased it in good faith in 1987, which was the kind of good faith that depended on not asking certain questions. She was in Vienna to ask them.'
  ]
) ON CONFLICT (style_slug) DO NOTHING;

